    "slug": "reading-ftc-data-disposal-guidance-for-support-teams",
    "title": "Reading FTC Data Disposal Guidance for Support Teams",
    "excerpt": "How a support lane can read FTC disposal material, build a practical deletion routine, and leave legal conclusions with the client.",
    "published": "2026-11-18",
    "updated": "2026-11-18",
    "statistic": "The Federal Trade Commission publishes guidance on disposing of consumer report information and on protecting personal information, including the principle that a business should dispose of information it no longer needs in a way that prevents unauthorized access. The agency's Disposal Rule applies to consumer report information and sets reasonable disposal requirements for the entities it covers.",
    "methodology": "This note reviews the FTC's Disposal Rule guidance, the agency's data disposal and privacy resources, the relevant part of the Code of Federal Regulations, and the FTC's guide to protecting personal information. It asks how a support lane can build a deletion routine that is usable and reviewable without deciding which legal regime applies to a client. The article does not provide legal advice.",
    "body": [
      "Begin with what the lane actually holds. A support team may keep downloaded reports, exported spreadsheets, scanned documents, screenshots, and email attachments. Each copy is a place where personal information can persist after the task ends. List the locations before writing a deletion rule, because a routine that misses a folder creates a false sense of security.",
      "Read the FTC material as a principle plus a specific rule. The broader guidance describes reasonable disposal as part of protecting personal information, while the Disposal Rule addresses consumer report information for the entities it covers. The two are related but not identical. Ask the client which obligation it believes applies and record the answer before designing the routine.",
      "Separate the record the client must keep from the working copy the lane no longer needs. Many tasks produce a temporary export that can be deleted as soon as the reviewed output is saved. Keeping every intermediate file because it might be useful increases exposure without adding value. Write the rule for the temporary copies first, because they are the most common source of leftovers.",
      "Choose a disposal method that matches the medium. A digital file may need more than a recycle-bin delete, depending on the device and the storage service. A paper document may need shredding rather than a trash can. Ask the client and the tool provider what method they consider reasonable, and record the answer for each medium the lane uses.",
      "Build the routine into the task rather than adding it as a monthly chore. When a report is approved, delete the draft export in the same session. When a screenshot is no longer needed, remove it from the device and the shared folder. A routine attached to the task is more reliable than a reminder that arrives after the files have multiplied.",
      "Name the person who performs each deletion and the person who checks it. A small team can use a shared checklist with a date and an initials field. The checker should confirm that the file is gone from the destination, not only that a request was sent. A deletion record without verification is a hope rather than a control.",
      "Handle shared folders and synced devices with care. Deleting a file from one device may leave a copy on another, in a sync service, or in a backup. Check the retention settings of the tools the lane uses and note where copies are kept. A routine that ignores sync and backup has not actually removed the information.",
      "Keep the evidence of disposal. Record the date, the record type, the method, and the person who approved the action. Store the evidence with the retention schedule rather than in the same folder as the deleted file. The evidence should be enough for a reviewer to see that the routine operated, without recreating the deleted content.",
      "Do not delete during a legal hold or an open investigation. A request to remove a file may conflict with the client's obligation to preserve it. Route the request to the client's owner and wait for a decision. Record the hold and the date it was lifted before resuming the routine.",
      "Review the tools that create copies automatically. A chat app may cache an attachment, a browser may keep a download, and a camera roll may sync a screenshot. Adjust the settings where possible and include the remaining copies in the disposal checklist. The least visible copy is often the one that survives the longest.",
      "Keep a simple inventory of the tools that hold personal information for the lane. Include the storage service, the chat app, the ticketing system, and any local download folder. Note the person who administers each tool and the retention setting in use. The inventory is the starting point for every disposal review because it shows where a copy can hide.",
      "Train the team on the difference between a working file and a record. A specialist who understands why a draft export is temporary is more likely to delete it without being reminded. Use one real example in onboarding and ask the person to identify which files can go. Repeat the training when a new tool enters the lane.",
      "Check the disposal method for the devices the team uses at home. A personal printer, a local download folder, or a phone camera roll can hold a copy that the office process never sees. Include those locations in the onboarding checklist and in the quarterly sample. A routine that covers only the shared drive leaves the most portable copies untouched.",
      "Review the tools' default retention settings after an update. A platform change can turn a thirty-day trash into a permanent archive or the reverse. Record the setting and the date it was checked, then confirm the behaviour with a test file. Defaults are a decision someone else made, not a policy the lane chose.",
      "Check the client's instructions before applying a general period. The client may require a longer retention for a contract, a tax rule, or an industry obligation. The FTC guidance is one input, not the whole schedule. Write the client's instruction beside the disposal rule so the team does not shorten a required period.",
      "Audit a sample each quarter. Pick a completed task and trace every file it produced, then confirm which were deleted and which were retained. Note the gaps and fix the path that created them. A sample of five tasks is enough to show whether the routine is working.",
      "Keep the disposal rule short enough to use. A one-page checklist with the locations, the methods, the owners, and the evidence field is more effective than a long policy nobody opens. Review it when a new system or a new client type appears. The rule should fit on the wall beside the work, not only in a compliance folder.",
      "Respond carefully to a client request for deletion. Confirm the scope, check for a hold or a retention requirement, and record the decision before acting. Tell the client what was deleted and what was retained, and why. A clear response prevents a later dispute about a file that was removed too quickly.",
      "State the limits in the account record. FTC guidance describes federal expectations for the entities and information it covers, and it does not replace the client's counsel, state law, industry rules, or a contractual retention term. The lane can run a disciplined disposal routine while the client owns the legal conclusion. Keep that boundary visible."
    ],
